    "answer": {
      "short": "$n+2=(n-2r)^2$, so $n+2$ is a perfect square.\n\nExpress the binomial coefficients using factorials and equate the consecutive differences.",
      "worked": "Using the factorial formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\binom{n}{r}-\\binom{n}{r-1}&=\\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}-\\frac{n!}{(r-1)!(n-r+1)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n}{r}-\\binom{n}{r-1}&=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!(r-1)!}\\left(\\frac1r-\\frac1{n-r+1}\\right).\n\\end{align*}$$\nSimilarly,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\binom{n}{r+1}-\\binom{n}{r}&=\\frac{n!}{(r+1)!(n-r-1)!}-\\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\\\\\n\\binom{n}{r+1}-\\binom{n}{r}&=\\frac{n!}{(n-r-1)!r!}\\left(\\frac1{r+1}-\\frac1{n-r}\\right).\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe consecutive differences are equal. Cancelling their common factorial factors gives\n$$\\frac1{n-r}\\left(\\frac1r-\\frac1{n-r+1}\\right)=\\frac1r\\left(\\frac1{r+1}-\\frac1{n-r}\\right).$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nr\\left(\\frac1r-\\frac1{n-r+1}\\right)&=(n-r)\\left(\\frac1{r+1}-\\frac1{n-r}\\right)\\\\\n1-\\frac{r}{n-r+1}&=\\frac{n-r}{r+1}-1\\\\\n\\frac{n-2r+1}{n-r+1}&=\\frac{n-2r-1}{r+1}\\\\\n(n-2r+1)(r+1)&=(n-2r-1)(n-r+1)\\\\\nnr+n-2r^2-r+1&=n^2-3nr+2r^2-r-1\\\\\nn+2&=n^2-4nr+4r^2\\\\\nn+2&=(n-2r)^2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n-2r$ is an integer, $n+2$ is a perfect square.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 145
